# National Symbols of India: The Flag, the Anthem, and the Rest

> A country announces itself through symbols. India's flag flies with a
> spinning wheel from a 2,300-year-old pillar capital; its anthem was sung
> by Rabindranath Tagore before the Crown knew it would one day become a
> republic's voice; its national song was born in a novel about monks
> fighting famine. Every general-knowledge paper since the 1950s has asked
> for one of these facts — and every one of them has a story attached.

## 1. The National Flag — the Tiranga

[definition]
The **National Flag of India** is a horizontal tricolour of deep saffron
(top), white (middle), and India green (bottom), in equal proportion, with
a navy-blue **Ashoka Chakra** (a 24-spoke wheel) at the centre of the
white band. Its design is based on a flag proposed by **Pingali
Venkayya** and adopted by the Constituent Assembly on **22 July 1947**.
[/definition]

The three bands carry meaning the exam expects you to recite: saffron
stands for courage and sacrifice, white for peace and truth, and green
for faith, fertility and prosperity. The Chakra, taken from the Lion
Capital of Sarnath, represents the **wheel of law (dharma)** and the
continuity of a moving, modern nation — 24 spokes, one for each hour of
the day, suggesting that a nation must move forward around the clock.

The flag's ratio is **3:2** (length to height). By the Flag Code of
India, 2002 — which replaced earlier restrictions — citizens may hoist
the flag on all days, subject to dignity and the code's rules: it must
not touch the ground or trail in water, and no other flag may fly above
it.

## 2. The Anthem, the Song, and the Emblem

| Symbol | Adopted | Author / Source | Key fact |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| National Anthem — *Jana Gana Mana* | 24 January 1950 | Rabindranath Tagore | Originally in Bengali; Hindi version is the official one |
| National Song — *Vande Mataram* | 24 January 1950 | Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay | From his 1882 novel *Anandamath* |
| National Emblem — Lion Capital | 26 January 1950 | Sculpture from Sarnath (c. 250 BCE) | *Satyameva Jayate* added below in Devanagari |
| National Calendar — Saka | 22 March 1957 | Reform Committee (chaired by Meghnad Saha) | 1 Chaitra = 22 March (21 in a leap year) |

*Jana Gana Mana* was first sung on **27 December 1911** at the Calcutta
session of the Indian National Congress. Its playing time is roughly
**52 seconds** for the full version — a favourite trick question, since
a shorter ~20-second version exists for specific occasions.
*Vande Mataram*, meanwhile, has equal status with the anthem but not the
same legal standing: Article 51A of the Constitution makes respecting
both a fundamental duty of every citizen, yet only the anthem has a
prescribed code of protocol.

The emblem shows three visible lions standing on a circular abacus,
with a bull, a horse, and the Dharma Chakra in between, and an inverted
lotus below. The motto **Satyameva Jayate** ("Truth alone triumphs") is
taken from the **Mundaka Upanishad**.

## 3. The Living Symbols — Flora, Fauna, and the River

| Symbol | Choice | Notes for exams |
| --- | --- | --- |
| National animal | Bengal tiger | Adopted 1973 with Project Tiger |
| National bird | Indian peafowl (peacock) | Declared 1963 |
| National flower | Lotus | *Nelumbo nucifera* |
| National tree | Banyan | Symbol of longevity |
| National fruit | Mango | National Research Centre in Lucknow |
| National river | Ganga | Declared the national river in 2008 |
| National aquatic animal | Ganges river dolphin | Declared 2009; blind, uses echolocation |
| National heritage animal | Indian elephant | Declared 2010 |

Two of these came late enough that coaching material often gets them
wrong: the Ganges river dolphin (2009) and the elephant (2010) are
recent designations aimed at conservation signalling. Project Tiger
(1973) began at Jim Corbett National Park with nine tiger reserves.

## 4. Identity Documents of the Republic

The **national calendar**, the Saka era, counts years from 78 CE and was
adopted on 22 March 1957 for official Gazette purposes alongside the
Gregorian calendar — the same day the reformed Indian astronomical
tables came into use. Its first month, Chaitra, begins on 22 March in a
common year and 21 March in a leap year.

The citizenship oath, the currency notes (which carry the emblem, the
anthem's opening, and the portrait of Mahatma Gandhi), and the official
seals of the Supreme Court and Parliament all reuse the Lion Capital —
making the emblem the most repeated piece of Mauryan sculpture in
history.

[example]
A previous-year paper asks: "The Ashoka Chakra of the national flag has
how many spokes?" The trap option is 12 (a clock face). The answer is
24. A second favourite: "Which Upanishad does *Satyameva Jayate* come
from?" — the trap is Ishavasya; the answer is Mundaka. When symbols
come in pairs (anthem and song, animal and heritage animal), the paper
almost always tests the pair you confuse.
[/example]

[summary]
India's national symbols were nearly all formalised in 1950: the
tricolour with the 24-spoke Ashoka Chakra (adopted 22 July 1947,
Pingali Venkayya's design), *Jana Gana Mana* by Tagore and *Vande
Mataram* by Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay, the Sarnath Lion Capital
emblem with *Satyameva Jayate* from the Mundaka Upanishad, and the Saka
calendar (from 1957). The tiger, peacock, lotus, banyan, mango and
Ganga are the classic picks; the Ganges river dolphin (2009) and Indian
elephant (2010) are the modern conservation-era additions every exam
loves to slip in.
[/summary]
